---
/** Spring's storm season and sewer lines: who owns what, and what to do. */
---

<section class="section section--soft" aria-labelledby="sn-h">
  <div class="wrap sn">
    <div>
      <h2 id="sn-h">When it pours in Spring</h2>
      <p>Heavy rain fills the sewer mains, and the first place it shows up is the lowest drain in the house. Stop running water, call your utility district if the neighbors are backing up too, and get your own line checked once it dries out.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/sewer-backs-up-when-it-rains">Why sewers back up in the rain</a></p>
    </div>
    <dl class="sn__who">
      <div><dt>Utility district (MUD)</dt><dd>The sewer main in the street and the water meter</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Homeowner, usually</dt><dd>The sewer line from the house to the main, and everything inside</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Not sure which?</dt><dd>A camera inspection shows where the blockage is</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .sn { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; }
  .sn__who { margin: 0; display: grid; gap: .75rem; }
  .sn__who div { background: #fff; border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .sn__who dt { font-weight: 800; }
  .sn__who dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  @media (min-width: 50rem) { .sn { grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 1fr; align-items: center; } }
</style>
